{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2606.00047, how can post-deployment monitoring help mitigate risks from both asset gain and continual learning?", "answer": "Post-deployment monitoring can help by tracking model use with restricted assets and detecting unsafe behavioral drift from continual learning.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "One solution is to conduct post-deployment monitoring or to work with national security authorities to improve elicitation stacks (see 4.1), though generalising results from such collaborations is difficult due to the idiosyncratic nature of restricted assets."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "Post-deployment monitoring could be a strategy to alleviate these risks (see 4.1), though much will depend on how continual learning is operationalised."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2606.00047, what governance approaches are recommended to address risks from both system gains and diffusion effects?", "answer": "The document recommends combining model-level governance with system, agent, and entity governance to address risks from system gains and diffusion effects.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "As argued below, these risks can be mitigated through changes to model-level governance in combination with alternative approaches such as entity, agent, and system governance."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "Model-level governance would remain useful to govern certain dangerous capabilities, but a broader suite of governance mechanisms would be required to manage diffusion risks, such as entity and agent governance (see 4.2.2 and 4.2.3)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2606.00047, what does 'asset gain' mean?", "answer": "Asset gain refers to capability increases from combining an AI model with special assets unavailable to original developers or testers, such as government-held expertise, specialised hardware, or classified data.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Asset gain refers to capability increases that result from combining an AI model with special assets not available to the original model developers or testers. These assets can include government-held expertise (e.g., advanced propulsion, stealth technology, nuclear physics), specialised hardware (e.g. physical embodiments, sensors, actuators), or classified data (e.g. undisclosed software vulnerabilities, data on non-public or enhanced pathogens, or sovereign decryption keys)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2606.00047, what is the purpose of Table 1 in section 4?", "answer": "Table 1 maps how different governance approaches may mitigate risks associated with non-model gains, showing which mechanisms address each capability paradigm.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "In Table 1, we attempt to map how different approaches may mitigate the risks associated with non-model gains. We reiterate that, while an exploration of different governance approaches is necessary, we do not expect model-level governance to become redundant."}]},
  {"question": "Which AI system is mentioned in arXiv 2606.00047 as the first LLM agent to discover a zero day vulnerability?", "answer": "Google DeepMind's Big Sleep is cited as the first LLM agent to discover a zero day vulnerability.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "A salient example is Google DeepMind's Big Sleep-a system integrating an LLM with tools like a code browser and debugger-which was the first LLM agent that discovered a zero day (Google Project Zero, 2024)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2606.00047, what is a risk of using transformer-based LLMs in robotics, as described in the embodiment gain section?", "answer": "A risk is that model hallucinations or jailbreaks could lead to physical safety issues when LLMs control robots.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Concerns about a model's susceptibility to hallucinations and jailbreaks may translate into physical safety risks (Robey et al., 2024; Cohen et al., 2024)."}]}
]}
